WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.760
OK, let's get in to our audience questions
for the week.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.050
There's a lot you can tell.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.879
We're getting close to this November election
and we are we're only about four months away

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:13.740
now because a lot of the questions coming
in from you guys are about the November election.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.390
The first question is about polling, and it's
about specifically comparing polling right

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.320
now to polling in 2016.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:26.890
The question is, David, how does Biden's lead
against Trump today differ from Hillary's

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.710
lead against Trump in 2016?

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:35.390
Or is this going to be a repeat of 2016 all
over again where Trump wins by a small margin

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.140
in a few states?

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.219
Excellent question.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:46.770
The first answer is we really don't know until
people vote, which means every single one

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:52.920
of you should be imagining my vote, could
determine whether Trump wins my state or whether

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.629
Biden wins my state.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:56.859
And you should go vote on that basis.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:00:58.079
That's number one.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:04.019
Now, let's look at the actual polls and look
at differences between Hillary's lead in 2016

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.350
and Trump's lead in 22 and Biden's lead in
2020.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:15.630
Hillary's popular vote lead in 2016 at the
time of the election was smaller than Biden's

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.200
is today.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.040
We've talked before about how it can happen
that you lose the popular vote by a lot and

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.259
win electorally.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:26.259
That can happen.

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:30.200
You can draw these maps where I think I saw
one over the weekend where you could have

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.670
twenty two percent of the popular vote and
still win the Electoral College.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.500
Just because you can make that scenario on
a map doesn't mean it's likely.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:45.450
And so one of the big differences between
2016 and 2020 is that right now, Biden's popular

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.689
vote lead is bigger than Hillary's was on
Election Day.

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:50.070
That can change.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:57.020
The second important difference between 2016
and 2020 is that when you look at some of

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:04.960
the states that are likely to be the key states,
the margin for Biden right now is far bigger.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:12.350
Hillary in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania and
Michigan, for example, in 2016.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.430
You look at Biden's Michigan numbers right
now.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:16.680
They're insane.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.690
So does that tell us they couldn't change
between now and November?

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.920
Of course, they could change what it tells
us as the polls would have to change far more

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.640
dramatically than we've typically seen them
change in the last four months.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.370
And or the polls would need to be way more
wrong this year than they've been in years

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.370
past.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.870
That's good for Joe Biden.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:45.910
The other sort of number to point to is that
Joe Biden is over 50 percent and well over

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.940
50 percent nationally in a lot of polls.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.960
Whereas at this point in 2016, Hillary Clinton
was hitting 50 in some polls.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.230
And when she did, it was usually like 50,
maybe 51.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.180
Joe Biden has regularly been hitting 50 percent
in polls.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.510
And the reason that that's extra important
right now is two reasons.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:14.970
One, if Biden's already at 50 in an important
state, for example, he doesn't need to worry

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.250
about the undecideds.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.250
And the third parties.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.280
I mean, yes, he still wants to try to get
them.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.870
But the point is, he as long as he retains
the people he currently has, can win those

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.450
states, even if against an incumbent.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:33.810
As you get closer and closer to Election Day,
more of the undecideds might break for the

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:34.810
incumbent.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:35.810
In this case, Donald Trump.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:39.000
So the 50 percent threshold is also important.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.330
And then that's the last thing this incumbency
factor.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:48.510
2016 was two challengers against each other
with an outgoing president.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:54.040
Now, you could make the case with an outgoing
Democratic president, Barack Obama.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.160
That is a help for the Democratic candidate,
Hillary Clinton.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.210
OK, that's not a factor right now.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.590
This is challenger versus incumbent.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:10.010
Now, in many ways, the power of incumbency
does suggest the undecideds as we get closer

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:15.310
to November, maybe some third party people,
maybe some people who like Trump but aren't

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:19.260
necessarily thinking of voting might decide
to vote.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.940
Usually incumbency helps you as you get closer
to Election Day.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:30.560
The concerning number, if you're Trump, is
that as an incumbent in some polls, he's in

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.820
the mid to high 30s.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.840
That's a very, very bad.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:41.430
And that that as an incumbent is a greater
red flag than if this was challenger against

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.430
Challenger.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:45.160
You look at Trump's high disapproval and you
sort of get an entire picture.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.020
So in hearing me talk about all of this, what
should you take away from it?

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.020
Nothing.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.940
Absolutely nothing you should be imagining.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:58.820
I could turn my state from Trump to Biden
if.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:04.930
I don't vote, that could be the vote that
gives my state to Trump if I do vote.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.270
My vote could be the vote that gives it to
buy it now.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.220
Rarely is it actually a one vote difference.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.380
But the point is, a lot of us have these thoughts.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:18.050
If all of us who have these thoughts decided
to stay home, Trump wins.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.810
And that's the most important takeaway that
I can imagine.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:28.180
Hopefully we will all make the right decisions
in November and hopefully November 4th will

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:32.500
be you know, it doesn't have to be the big
progressive victory that we would all like.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:37.260
It would still be a major, major improvement
in the United States.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:37.990
And I hope to be there with you when it happens.

